WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.760
Electrophilic aromatic substitution can be
used to introduce a nitro group onto an aromatic

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.760
ring.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:16.119
The nitro group is an interesting functional
group because no matter how we draw the Lewis

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:20.310
structure, we cannot avoid having formal charges
on it.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:25.610
Now if we just focus on the extra electron
pair that's making the formal negative charge

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:31.310
on this single bonded oxygen—we can actually
represent this as the negative charge—and

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:37.200
then see how the movement of this electron
pair to make a new double bond here can push

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.290
electrons onto the other oxygen, giving it
the formal negative charge.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:47.371
We can show our delocalization a bit better
by showing this representation; however, this

00:00:47.371 --> 00:00:50.460
is not convenient for showing arrow pushing.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:55.950
In the nitration reaction, benzene can be
reacted with a mixture of sulfuric and nitric

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.780
acid to form nitrobenzene.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:05.710
The mechanism begins with nitric and sulfuric
acid reacting to form the nitronium ion.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.390
In the first step, the stronger acid protonates
the weaker acid.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.859
But, wait a second...

00:01:10.859 --> 00:01:14.609
nitric and sulfuric acid are both strong acids.

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:16.740
So how can we tell which is stronger?

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:23.490
Well, we can look at a pKa table and see that
nitric acid has a pKa of -1.3 and sulfuric

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.990
acid has a pKa of -3.0.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.990
But we can also look at stabilizing effects
in the conjugate bases.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:36.960
Remember, when the conjugate base of any acid
is more stabilized, the compound is more likely

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.649
to give up a proton because it has stabilization
when that proton is gone.

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:46.700
Let's take a moment to look at the resonance
forms of nitric and sulfuric acid, and see

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.359
if we can rationalize sulfuric acid being
the stronger acid.

00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:56.609
We can show delocalization in the conjugate
base of nitric acid by pushing electrons from

00:01:56.609 --> 00:02:01.069
the oxygen anion through the double bonded
oxygen.

00:02:01.069 --> 00:02:06.039
This gives us a total of three resonance forms
for the conjugate base of nitric acid.

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:09.229
Now, let's look at the conjugate base of sulfuric
acid.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:13.879
Here, we're just showing the first proton
coming off, because that is the one that ionizes

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:14.879
completely.

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:16.629
The second one does not.

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:21.769
In each of these forms, we're going to maintain
six bonds to sulfur, but we're going to push

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:28.150
electrons so that we have our oxyanions forming
sulfur–oxygen double bonds.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:33.959
We can see for the conjugate base of sulfuric
acid, we also have three resonance forms.

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:35.689
So, what's the difference?

00:02:35.689 --> 00:02:38.680
Why is sulfuric acid a stronger acid?

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.930
In the conjugate base of sulfuric acid, we
actually have another effect going on.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.050
This inductive electron withdrawing effect
further stabilizes the negative charge in

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:56.879
the conjugate base of sulfuric acid, making
this more stabilized and thus a stronger acid.

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:01.989
Returning to our mechanism, we can now show
nitric acid getting protonated by sulfuric

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:02.989
acid.

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:07.269
If we protonate the oxygen containing the
hydrogen atom, this will make water, which

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:08.640
is a good leaving group.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.540
So, the product of this reaction is a protonated
form of nitric acid and the conjugate base

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.959
of sulfuric acid, which we looked at in detail
below.

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:23.280
Now, we have two formal positive charges next
to each other in this molecule.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.270
So, we want to relieve this repulsive force,
and break off water.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:34.189
However, if we just made water leave, we would
end up with a 2+ charge on nitrogen, which

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:36.239
isn't very favorable at all.

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:42.459
Fortunately, we have that oxyanion right next
door that can push down its electrons assisting

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:48.640
in the departure of the leaving group and
leaving nitrogen with just a +1 charge.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.409
Elimination of the leaving group gives the
nitronium ion, and a molecule of water.

00:03:54.409 --> 00:04:00.439
Now, the nitronium ion is a powerful electrophile,
and so benzene will open up its double bond

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:02.170
and attack it.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:06.639
Now we have a carbocation on benzene that
needs to be neutralized.

00:04:06.639 --> 00:04:10.719
It can be neutralized by the deprotonation
of this hydrogen atom.

00:04:10.719 --> 00:04:15.359
Now, in this mechanism, it might be tempting
to take the conjugate base of sulfuric acid

00:04:15.359 --> 00:04:21.539
to do this deprotonation; however, remember
how resonance stabilized that was from above?

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:26.990
It's not going to be a very good nucleophile
because its electrons are all tied up in that

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.340
resonance and that inductive effect.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.020
So, there's a better nucleophile—a better
base—in this solution that we can use for

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.330
the deprotonation.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:38.500
Notice, we made a molecule of water above.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:44.020
Also, even the most concentrated acids have
a significant amount of water in them.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:49.860
So water is a much better base to use in this
deprotonation, and gives us nitrobenzene plus

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.779
a molecule of hydronium ion.

